  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 048: More accelerators help only when the useful work they add justifies the extra communication, waiting and recovery cost.

Lesson 049: Dense AI racks need enough electricity and cooling at every step, or expensive chips cannot run as intended.

Lesson 050: Reliable building power needs independent, adequately sized paths from the site connection to each rack.

Lesson 051: Contracted electricity becomes useful only after a completed and tested grid connection can deliver it to the servers.

Lesson 052: Cooling must carry heat from each chip all the way outside, or powered equipment cannot sustain full performance.

Everything is installed. Why is the cluster not ready?

Imagine a new 32-server AI cluster. This is an illustrative deployment. The racks are energized, coolant is flowing, every server boots and the network switches show active links. Yet the first 32-server training test runs slowly and sometimes stops.

The problem is not a missing major component. Two network links are operating below their intended speed, one server has different firmware and the storage path stalls when all workers read data together. Each subsystem appeared healthy when checked alone. The combined system is not yet dependable.

Commissioning is the planned process of proving and documenting that installed equipment performs as intended. Integration connects the hardware, software and operating procedures so the parts behave as one usable system. Delivery puts equipment in the room; commissioning turns that equipment into evidence-backed capacity.

Define “ready” before testing

A useful acceptance plan states what the system must do and under which conditions. It might require every server to be visible, power and cooling to remain within limits, network and storage performance to meet agreed thresholds, representative jobs to complete consistently and specified failures to be handled safely.

Without those criteria, “the cluster works” can mean only that someone saw lights and login screens. Clear thresholds also separate a defect from an expectation that was never agreed.

NVIDIA's data-center guidance describes deployment as coordination across facilities, data-center operations, networking, security, application owners and other teams. A change in one domain can alter another: spreading racks because of a power limit can change cable lengths and network performance. Commissioning therefore needs one owner for the end-to-end result, even when many vendors own individual parts.

Test the facility before risking the servers

Power and cooling systems are first checked as components: breakers operate, pumps move the intended flow, sensors report correctly and controls respond. Teams then test connected behavior under load.

A load bank is equipment that safely imitates electrical demand. It lets qualified commissioning teams apply a known load without depending on production servers. They can observe voltage, temperature, airflow or coolant behavior and run planned failure scenarios, such as loss of one power path, to see whether the surviving system behaves as designed.

This proves the facility can support an IT-like load at the tested boundary. It does not prove that GPUs can communicate, storage can feed them or software can recover a distributed job. Facility acceptance and computing acceptance are connected but different.

